![Vector diagram](images/b8/b87f77266a2d44642d01744a5bdcecf42345dd5ffd26531eae5af7c77bea2722.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

solution:
![Completed vector route](images/90/9013b552fbf1df93e6872f3fd213f1c80e3d4c1e935dd2317f04d52b69d3cdd2.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $12$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares up. Thus $OX=3|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-3\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $3$ squares left and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. Thus $XC=\frac{3}{2}|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-3\mathbf{b}+\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{a}\\&=\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{a}-3\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=\frac{3}{2}$ and $\mu=-3$.

